# gateway.env — where YOUR gateway lives. Copy this file to gateway.env
# (same directory) and edit. gateway.env is gitignored; this template is not.
#
#     cp gateway.env.example gateway.env
#
# Nothing here is required. Every value has a default, and every script still
# accepts its usual command-line argument and environment variable, which win
# over this file. Set a key only to stop repeating yourself.
#
# Where the defaults come from when a key is absent, in order:
#   1. what the last install wrote to .gateway-state (also gitignored)
#   2. a value derived from THIS machine's LAN — the address the host uses,
#      its netmask, and the router from its default route
#   3. the project's historic constants (192.168.1.88 / 192.168.1.6)
# So on a 192.168.0.0/24 network the scripts propose 192.168.0.88, not an
# address you cannot reach.

# --- the gateway running Linux ----------------------------------------------

# Its address. Also used as the proposal at the static-IP prompt when building
# an image, so a re-install keeps the same address instead of re-deriving one.
#GW_IP=192.168.1.88

# Netmask and default gateway written into /userdata/etc/eth0.conf at install
# time. Left unset, they follow this host's own LAN.
#GW_NETMASK=255.255.255.0
#GW_GATEWAY=192.168.1.1

# The name the gateway answers to. It sends this in its DHCP request, so most
# home routers resolve it — which is what makes DHCP mode workable for the
# tooling. Defaults to the hostname shipped in the userdata skeleton.
#GW_HOST=rtl8196e-gw

# --- the gateway in bootloader mode -----------------------------------------

# The bootloader has no DHCP client: it comes up on a fixed address, handed to
# it by boothold. It MUST be on the same L2 segment as this host — the TFTP
# transfer that flashes the image does not cross a router.
#BOOT_IP=192.168.1.6

# --- a note on DHCP ---------------------------------------------------------
#
# DHCP mode is the absence of /userdata/etc/eth0.conf on the device; there is
# no address to record, and it can change. Two ways to keep the tooling working:
#
#   - Reserve a lease by MAC on your router, then set GW_IP to the reserved
#     address. The gateway generates its MAC once and keeps it in
#     /userdata/etc/mac_address, which survives reboots and full flashes, so the
#     reservation holds. This is the recommended setup: DHCP on the box, a fixed
#     address for the scripts.
#   - Or rely on the name (GW_HOST above) and leave GW_IP unset.
